insert into public.web_guide_knowledge (key, part, title, body, link, sort, source) values
('about', 'Opening', 'Who we are', $k$Grupo Soltura (Soltura Real Estate) is a local real estate agency on the Costa Blanca South, active since 2002, member no. 992 of the API college of Alicante and registered in the Valencian register of agents (RAICV 5781). Office: Avenida de la Constitución s/n, 03179 Formentera del Segura, between Rojales and Guardamar del Segura. Office hours Monday to Friday 10:00-14:00; Saturday and Sunday by appointment. WhatsApp +34 664 414 515, phone +34 966 792 113, info@gruposoltura.com. The team: Rick, Marco, Ingrid and Lisette. We speak Dutch, English, German and Spanish (no other languages). We guide the whole purchase ourselves, from the first viewing to the notary and the months after. Always a step ahead: first with drone footage, our own app and property info by SMS, and today automatic weekly updates for sellers.$k$, 'contact', 10, 'site/privacy + business facts'),
('guide', 'Opening', 'The guide itself', $k$Our free guide "At home on the Costa Blanca" (Dutch: "Thuis aan de Costa Blanca", Spanish: "En casa en la Costa Blanca") is a full-colour PDF in Dutch, English or Spanish, sent by email. Seven parts: 1 think first, then look (the 10 questions, holiday home / rental / emigration); 2 where do you want to live (the Vega Baja town by town, climate, airports); 3 what does it really cost (three cost layers: price, purchase costs, yearly costs; mortgages); 4 type of home (apartment, bungalow, villa, new build, renting out); 5 the purchase step by step (NIE, reservation, deposit contract, notary, legal checks, the 12 costliest mistakes); 6 your viewing trip; 7 after the keys (utilities, taxes as owner, Spanish will, moving with your family, pets, life here). It ends with a "ready to buy?" checklist. Prices of our services are not in the guide: they are on request.$k$, 'guide', 20, 'TOC woon/koopgids 23-10-2026'),
('ten_questions', '1 Think first', 'The questions to ask yourself first', $k$Before viewing anything, be clear about: 1 what the home is for (holiday home, renting out, emigrating, or a mix); 2 your total budget including purchase costs, not just the price; 3 how many weeks a year you will be there; 4 coast or inland (beach walking distance costs more; inland gives more space for the money); 5 must-haves versus nice-to-haves (bedrooms, private or communal pool, terrace, solarium, parking, garden); 6 how close to an airport, shops, a health centre and golf; 7 stairs and accessibility for the coming 10-20 years; 8 cash or mortgage, and whether you need to sell something first; 9 if you plan to rent out: is it allowed for that home (see renting); 10 how long you plan to keep it. Visit outside the summer too: life in January is different from August.$k$, 'buy', 30, null),
('region', '2 Where to live', 'Our region: the Vega Baja / Costa Blanca South', $k$We work in the Vega Baja del Segura at the south of the province of Alicante: Rojales (with Ciudad Quesada, Doña Pepa and La Marquesa), Formentera del Segura, Benijófar, Daya Nueva, Daya Vieja, Almoradí, Dolores, Catral, Algorfa, Los Montesinos, San Miguel de Salinas, San Fulgencio (La Marina), Guardamar del Segura, Torrevieja (La Mata) and Orihuela Costa (La Zenia, Playa Flamenca, Cabo Roig, Campoamor). Coastal towns: beaches, promenades, more tourism and higher prices per m². Inland villages: Spanish village life, markets, more house and plot for the money, usually 10-20 minutes to the beach. Alicante airport is typically 35-50 minutes by car, Murcia (Corvera) airport about 30-45 minutes. Mild winters, hot summers (July and August are very hot), lots of sunshine. Natural spots: the dunes and pine forest of Guardamar, the pink and salt lakes of Torrevieja and La Mata (natural park). We have a detailed living guide per town on our website (schools, healthcare, shops, fiestas, distances).$k$, 'area', 40, 'web_town_guides'),
('budget', '3 Costs', 'What you get for your budget', $k$We do not publish price indications in the guide, because the market moves. The live answer is our current offer: the assistant can search our homes by town, type, budget, bedrooms and pool, and the website shows how a price compares to the market. As a rule: inland villages and urbanisations give the most space per euro, front-line and walking-distance-to-beach homes are the most expensive, new build costs more than comparable resale but comes with guarantees and modern standards.$k$, 'buy', 50, null),
('costs_purchase', '3 Costs', 'Purchase costs (layers 1 and 2)', $k$Think in three layers: 1 the price; 2 the one-off purchase costs; 3 the yearly costs. Purchase taxes in the Comunitat Valenciana: resale home: transfer tax (ITP) of {itp}% of the price (lowered from 10% to 9% by Valencian law 5/2025); new build bought from the developer: VAT (IVA) {iva}% plus stamp duty (AJD) {ajd}%. On top come notary, land registry and, depending on your situation, NIE, power of attorney, mortgage costs and legal guidance. Grupo Soltura does not work a la carte: our guidance is one all-in fee agreed up front, on request. Ask us for a personal cost overview for a specific home: we give the exact figure before anything is signed. Figures are always indicative.$k$, 'buy', 60, 'web_config; Ley 5/2025 GVA'),
('costs_yearly', '3 Costs', 'Yearly costs as owner (layer 3)', $k$Yearly costs: IBI (municipal property tax, based on the cadastral value), waste collection tax (basura), community fees if the home is in a community of owners (pool, gardens), home insurance, utilities (electricity, water, internet), and for non-residents the yearly non-resident income tax (Modelo 210): on a home you do not rent out, the tax office assumes an income of 1.1% of the cadastral value (2% if the cadastral value was not recently revised), taxed at 19% for EU/EEA residents and 24% for others; rental income is declared separately. We can file Modelo 210 for you (on request). The website has a monthly costs calculator for people moving here.$k$, 'costs', 70, 'AEAT Modelo 210'),
('mortgage', '3 Costs', 'Financing and mortgages', $k$Spanish banks lend to non-residents, usually up to about 60-70% of the purchase price or valuation (whichever is lower); residents can often get more. You need your own funds for the rest plus all purchase costs. The bank asks for ID, NIE, proof of income (payslips or accounts, tax returns), bank statements, existing debts and a credit check; you need a Spanish bank account. Fixed and variable rates exist; variable rates follow the 12-month Euribor (currently about {euribor}%). We arrange mortgage mediation with Spanish banks (fee on request). Get a mortgage in principle before you start viewing seriously.$k$, 'buy', 80, 'web_config'),
('types', '4 Type of home', 'Types of homes', $k$Our homes are one of nine types: villa (detached, own plot, often a private pool), apartment, penthouse (top floor, usually a big terrace), bungalow (ground-floor or top-floor unit in a low-rise complex, typical for the Costa Blanca), quadrant / quad house (four homes in one block, each with its own garden or terrace), semi-detached, townhouse (terraced, often on two floors), duplex and finca (country house with land). Pools: none, private or communal (shared in the urbanisation). Resale is often immediately available, cheaper per m² and in established areas; new build has a 10-year structural guarantee, modern insulation and energy standards, but you buy from plans and wait for completion.$k$, 'buy', 90, null),
('newbuild', '4 Type of home', 'New build: how it works with us', $k$For new-build projects we always start with personal contact before sending any information (prices, plans, brochures or payment schedules), and preferably a visit to the project together. For that we need your first and last name, mobile, email and when you could come to Spain. How buying new build works: a reservation fee takes the unit off the market, then a purchase contract with staged payments during construction; by Spanish law those payments must be protected by a bank guarantee or insurance (ask for it, every time); at completion you pay the rest at the notary and get the keys after the first-occupation documents are in place. The builder gives a 10-year structural guarantee. We check the developer's guarantees and accompany the handover (snagging).$k$, 'buy', 100, 'business facts 22-10-2026'),
('renting', '4 Type of home', 'Renting out', $k$Holiday (tourist) rental in the Comunitat Valenciana requires registration of the home in the Valencian tourism register, and since July 2025 also a national single registration number for platforms such as Airbnb and Booking. Since April 2025 a community of owners can decide (by a 3/5 majority) whether new tourist rentals are allowed in the building or complex, and towns can set their own limits. Long-term rental falls under the Spanish tenancy law (LAU) and is simpler. Rental income must be declared (Modelo 210 for non-residents). Never buy on the assumption that you can rent out: we check per home what is allowed before you commit.$k$, 'buy', 110, 'Decreto-ley 9/2024 GVA; RD 1312/2024; LO 1/2025'),
('future_proof', '4 Type of home', 'Buying future-proof', $k$Think 10-20 years ahead: stairs to the bedrooms or solarium, distance to shops, a health centre and a bus, the energy certificate (mandatory for selling; rating A to G), orientation (south-facing gets winter sun, in summer shade matters), air-conditioning with heat pump for winter, and whether the community is well managed (ask for the latest minutes and the budget).$k$, 'buy', 120, null),
('process', '5 Purchase', 'The purchase step by step', $k$1 Search and viewings (we plan them, also by video). 2 Offer: we negotiate with the seller. 3 Reservation: a reservation deposit takes the home off the market while the legal checks are done. 4 Legal checks (see legal checks). 5 Private purchase contract (contrato de arras): usually 10% of the price is paid; if the buyer withdraws he loses it, if the seller withdraws he pays it back double. 6 Notary: the deed (escritura) is signed and the rest is paid; you get the keys. 7 Afterwards: taxes paid, the deed registered in the land registry, utilities and IBI put in your name. For resale bought in cash this often takes around 6 to 10 weeks; with a mortgage or for new build it takes longer. You do not have to be in Spain for every step: with a power of attorney we can sign for you.$k$, 'buy', 130, null),
('nie_bank_poa', '5 Purchase', 'NIE, Spanish bank account and power of attorney', $k$NIE: the tax identification number for foreigners, needed to buy, pay taxes and connect utilities. Apply in Spain (police / immigration office, by appointment) or at a Spanish consulate in your home country, or via a representative with a power of attorney. Spanish bank account: needed for the purchase payments, direct debits for IBI, community and utilities, and for a mortgage. Power of attorney (poder notarial): signed at a Spanish notary or at a Spanish consulate (or at a local notary with an apostille and translation); it lets a trusted person sign at the notary for you, so you do not need to fly in for every step. We arrange all three as part of our guidance.$k$, 'buy', 140, null),
('legal_checks', '5 Purchase', 'Legal checks before you sign', $k$Before money changes hands we check: the land registry extract (nota simple): who owns it and whether there are mortgages, seizures or other charges; that what is on paper (land registry and cadastre) matches reality, including extensions, pools and closed-in terraces (illegal additions are a common problem); building legality and the first-occupation / habitability documents; that IBI, waste tax and community fees are paid (certificate of the community); the energy certificate; and for new build the licences and the bank guarantees. Any debt stays with the property, so these checks protect you.$k$, 'buy', 150, null),
('under_offer', '5 Purchase', 'What "under offer" / reserved means with us', $k$When a home of ours is marked reserved (under offer), a buyer has reserved it: we stop viewings and expect the sale to complete. If the sale falls through, the home comes back on the market. On the home's page you can ask to be told when it comes back ("Back on the market" button), and we are happy to show similar homes.$k$, 'buy', 160, 'business facts 18-10-2026'),
('mistakes', '5 Purchase', 'The costliest mistakes and how to avoid them', $k$1 Budgeting only the price, not the taxes and costs. 2 Paying a deposit without a written contract or to a private account without guarantees. 3 Skipping the legal checks. 4 Buying illegal extensions or a pool not on the deeds. 5 Assuming you can rent out. 6 Ignoring community rules and fees. 7 Choosing a location from a summer holiday only. 8 Underestimating stairs, distances and car dependence. 9 No NIE, bank account or power of attorney ready, which delays the notary. 10 Buying new build without a bank guarantee. 11 Not making a Spanish will. 12 Forgetting the yearly Modelo 210 as non-resident.$k$, 'buy', 170, null),
('viewing_trip', '6 Viewing trip', 'Your viewing trip', $k$Tell us beforehand: your wishes, budget and how you will finance, the dates you are here, and who decides. We then select homes and plan a viewing day (usually 6-10 homes is the maximum that stays meaningful), with the towns in between so you also get a feel for the area. Bring your passport (for an NIE and a possible reservation), proof of funds or a mortgage in principle, and comfortable shoes. Best periods: spring and autumn; July and August are hot. You are welcome at our office in Formentera del Segura (Monday to Friday 10:00-14:00, weekends by appointment). Plan the trip via the contact page or WhatsApp.$k$, 'contact', 180, null),
('after_keys', '7 After the keys', 'After the keys: utilities, insurance and taxes', $k$After the notary: electricity and water contracts in your name (water is often organised per town), internet, home insurance (a mortgage bank will require it), IBI and community fees by direct debit, and as non-resident the yearly Modelo 210. Selling later: as a non-resident seller the buyer withholds 3% of the price for the tax office as advance on the capital gains tax, and the town charges the plusvalía municipal tax. We stay your point of contact after the purchase.$k$, 'buy', 190, null),
('will', '7 After the keys', 'A Spanish will', $k$A Spanish will that covers your Spanish assets makes inheritance in Spain much simpler and faster for your heirs, and under EU rules you can choose the law of your nationality. It is signed at a Spanish notary. We arrange it on request.$k$, 'buy', 200, null),
('emigrate', '7 After the keys', 'Moving here with your family', $k$EU citizens: register at the town hall (padrón), and when you stay longer than three months apply for the EU registration certificate (residencia) with your passport, NIE, padrón and proof of health cover and sufficient means. You become a Spanish tax resident if you spend more than 183 days a year here or your main interests are here. Healthcare: via Spanish social security when you work here or with an EU pension form (S1), otherwise private insurance. Schools: Spanish public and semi-private schools (free, in Spanish and Valencian) and several international schools within reach of our towns. Non-EU citizens need a visa (for example non-lucrative or digital nomad); the Spanish golden visa ended in April 2025. Our Move to Spain page and monthly costs calculator help with the numbers.$k$, 'move', 210, null),
('pets', '7 After the keys', 'Bringing pets', $k$Dogs, cats and ferrets travelling within the EU need a microchip, an EU pet passport and a valid rabies vaccination (given at least 21 days before travel). In Spain dogs must be registered in the regional pet register through a vet, and Spain's animal welfare law sets rules on care and leaving animals alone. Check airline rules early.$k$, 'move', 220, null),
('life', '7 After the keys', 'Life here', $k$Weekly street markets in almost every town, tapas and rice dishes, Moors and Christians and patron-saint fiestas, Holy Week processions and the San Juan bonfires in June, golf courses all around (La Marquesa in Ciudad Quesada and many more on the coast), beaches from Guardamar to Orihuela Costa, walking and cycling along the Segura river and in the salt-lake natural park of Torrevieja and La Mata. Our town guides list the market days, festivals and distances per town.$k$, 'area', 230, null),
('selling', 'Services', 'Selling with us', $k$For sellers: a free valuation based on real market data, professional photos and drone images, publication on our own website, on portals such as Idealista and thinkSPAIN and with our partner agents, viewings handled by us, and an automatic weekly update with the statistics of your home (how often it was found, viewed and saved). Then reservation, contract and notary as for buyers. Our commission is on request. Request a valuation on the Selling page.$k$, 'sell', 240, 'business facts'),
('services', 'Services', 'Our services', $k$Buyer guidance from search to keys (one all-in fee, agreed up front), mortgage mediation, NIE and power of attorney, Modelo 210 filings, Spanish wills, and selling your home. All fees are on request: ask us via the contact page or WhatsApp and we send a personal overview.$k$, 'contact', 250, null),
('words', '7 After the keys', 'Handy Spanish words', $k$escritura = title deed; nota simple = land registry extract; notario = notary; registro de la propiedad = land registry; catastro = cadastre; IBI = property tax; basura = waste tax; comunidad de propietarios = community of owners; arras = deposit contract; NIE = foreigner tax number; empadronamiento / padrón = town registration; plusvalía = municipal capital gains tax; obra nueva = new build; reventa = resale; piscina comunitaria = communal pool.$k$, null, 260, null)
